import json
from pathlib import Path
from docxlib import h1, h2, h3, p, li, caption, table, note, figcap

R = json.loads((Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "analysis" / "results.json").read_text())
TF = R["gh_phases"]["Full create"]["mean"] / 60
MAN = R["sim_manual"]["mean"] / 60
RATIO = R["h1"]["ratio"]
DES = R["gh_phases"]["Full destroy"]["mean"] / 60
K = R["man_rebuild_success"]["k"]
N = R["n_tf"]
G = R["gh_phases"]
CV = G["Full create"]["cv_pct"]
TRG = R["trigger"]["Full create"]
CYC = G["Total cycle"]["mean"] / 60

BLOCKS = [
h1("CHAPTER 5: DISCUSSION"),
h2("5.1 Introduction"),
p("This chapter interprets the results reported in Chapter 4. It takes each research question in turn, relating the quantitative results and the themes to the literature reviewed in Chapter 2, then draws meta-inferences from the integrated evidence, revisits the conceptual framework, compares the findings with prior studies, and discusses the findings that were not anticipated. Throughout, the discussion keeps separate what was measured (the Terraform pipeline), what was simulated (manual provisioning) and what could not be tested."),
h2("5.2 Version-Control Coverage and Current Provisioning (RQ1)"),
p("Moving the architecture from the AWS console into Terraform raised version-control coverage from nothing to 79.2% of components fully defined, and the remaining gaps are informative. Two of the five undefined or partial components are security controls at the network edge: the Web Application Firewall and TLS on the load balancer. This resembles the pattern Verdet et al. (2025) found across 812 Terraform projects, in which access-control policies were applied consistently while encryption was neglected. Here encryption at rest was applied (the state bucket and lock table are encrypted) but encryption in transit was not. The likely explanation, supported by Theme 1, is that both controls depend on something outside the code: a registered domain and certificate for TLS, and an account plan that permits the relevant services for Inspector. Components that need an external prerequisite are the ones that stay manual."),
p("The more consequential finding is that coverage and review came apart. Every change was in the repository, yet none was reviewed. This is the distinction the conceptual framework drew between the file format and the review gate, and the data show it is not hypothetical: a single engineer working at speed produced a complete version history with no review at all. For a regulated institution, the history alone would satisfy the requirement that changes be recorded, but not the requirement that they be approved."),
h2("5.3 Effects of the Provisioning Method (RQ2)"),
h3("5.3.1 Provisioning Duration (H1)"),
p(f"The pipeline built the full environment, 140 resources and five running services, in {TF:.1f} minutes with a coefficient of variation below 5%. The simulated manual build took {MAN:.1f} minutes, {RATIO:.1f} times longer, and the sensitivity analysis showed that the direction of the difference holds even if each resource took only 20 seconds in the console. H1 is therefore supported, with the important qualification that the manual side of the comparison is a model: the measured contribution of this study is the Terraform figure and its consistency, while the size of the gap depends on how fast a real engineer would be. The comparison is consistent with what the literature implies (Regvart et al., 2026) and with the practical observation that most of a manual build is hands-on console work, which Terraform removes entirely, whereas the waiting time for NAT gateways, load balancers and services is paid by both methods."),
p(f"The comparison with Regvart et al. (2026) is instructive. Their Terraform build of a smaller multi-tier architecture took 253 s; the shared infrastructure stack here, which contains a comparable network with two NAT gateways and a load balancer, took about {G['Create infrastructures']['mean']:.0f} s, and the full build took about twice their figure because it adds five service stacks and the time for containers to become healthy. Their standard deviation of 8.42 s (a coefficient of variation of about 3%) is of the same order as the {CV:.1f}% observed here over the same number of cycles, thirty, which suggests that low variability is a property of Terraform on AWS rather than of a particular architecture. Consistency is what makes environments disposable: a build that takes eight minutes every time can be scheduled, automated and trusted."),
p(f"The stability checks add a nuance that a smaller sample would have hidden. The sixteen back-to-back dispatched runs built about {TRG['dispatch']['mean'] - TRG['push']['mean']:.0f} s ({(TRG['test']['ratio'] - 1) * 100:.1f}%) slower than the push-triggered runs, while the destroy phases and the total cycle did not differ. The steps executed were identical, so the difference is best read as the influence of conditions outside the code, such as AWS API latency and the load on shared GitHub runners at a given time. Variation of this size is the floor that any IaC timing study inherits from the cloud platform; it is small beside the differences between provisioning methods, but it means that timing comparisons between tools or methods should interleave the conditions in time rather than run each in a separate block, so that platform conditions do not masquerade as a tool effect."),
h3("5.3.2 Configuration Drift (H2)"),
p("H2 could not be tested, and the reason is itself a finding. A pre-production environment was created during development and then destroyed, so when measurement began only one environment existed. Theme 2 shows why: every environment carries cost (NAT gateways, a load balancer and Fargate tasks run continuously) and state to manage, and a single engineer under time pressure kept only the one that was needed. The code inspection gives a structural reason to expect low drift, since environment differences are confined to the workspace name, but structure is not measurement. Kolomytskyi et al. (2026) argue that drift must be detected continuously and classified by severity; the present study shows the practical precondition for that, which is keeping more than one environment alive."),
h3("5.3.3 Reproducibility (H3)"),
p(f"Every one of the {N} measured pipeline rebuilds succeeded and all {R['n_starts']} service start-ups came back into service, consistent with Regvart et al. (2026), who reported no failed deployment in ninety. With thirty cycles the lower bound of the 95% interval for the pipeline's success rate is {R['tf_rebuild_success']['low'] * 100:.0f}%, so a failure rate above about one in nine can be ruled out for this procedure. In the simulation, only {K} of 10 manual rebuilds reproduced the baseline exactly, because recreating 140 resources by hand gives many opportunities for a small slip; even at a per-resource error rate of 0.2%, about a quarter of manual rebuilds would differ. H3 is therefore supported conditional on the model. Two qualifications apply. The Terraform measure shows that the environment came back and worked, not that every attribute was identical, and Rahman et al. (2020) identified idempotency defects as a distinct category of IaC fault that an attribute-level comparison would be needed to rule out. And Theme 2 shows that the rebuild was reproducible because its author engineered around Terraform's state problem; reproducibility was a designed property of the procedure, not a free consequence of using Terraform."),
h3("5.3.4 Version-Control Coverage and Review (H4)"),
p("H4 was partially supported. The coverage half moved from 0% to 79.2%; the review half did not move at all. Hilton et al. (2016) and Vasilescu et al. (2015) associate CI with faster and better-quality contribution, but in their open-source settings pull requests are the normal route for change. In a single-engineer setting with no branch protection, direct pushes to the main branch are the path of least resistance, and the pipeline was in fact triggered by direct pushes, fifteen of which existed only to start a run. When repetition became tedious, the response was more automation (a repeat option that chained sixteen runs), not more review. Decan et al. (2022) found push to be the most common GitHub Actions trigger (63.4% of repositories), ahead of pull requests (56.3%); this study shows what a push-triggered infrastructure pipeline does to review."),
h2("5.4 The Role of Pipeline Integration Depth (RQ3)"),
p("H5 could not be tested because drift was not measured and only one integration level was observed. The qualitative evidence nonetheless speaks to the construct. Moving infrastructure into a GitHub Actions pipeline added automation, timing, reporting and a shared record of every run, but no approval. Kolomytskyi et al. (2026) report that a gated, policy-checked workflow caught all violations before apply while a plain pipeline caught half; the present study cannot confirm that, but it shows that the step from pipeline execution to gated apply does not happen by itself. A gated workflow would also cost time: it must separate plan from apply so that a reviewer can read the plan, adding a planning step and the reviewer's own time to every change."),
h2("5.5 Barriers and Enablers (RQ4)"),
p("Four themes answer RQ4. The first enabler was that the architecture could be redesigned cheaply: because it was code, the move from per-service load balancers and gateways to shared ones could be made in stages over an afternoon and applied across five services at once. The first barrier is the one Rostami Mazrae et al. (2023) predicted: their respondents described a commit-push-wait loop caused by the inability to test workflows locally, and the record here shows the same loop, including a failed report commit that was only discoverable by running the pipeline."),
p("The second barrier, state, is not prominent in the reviewed literature, which concentrates on code quality, security and dependencies (Begoug et al., 2023; Rahman, Mahdavi-Hezaveh, & Williams, 2019). In practice it consumed a large share of the engineering effort: state files committed to Git with thousand-line diffs, a remote-state stack that cannot store its own state remotely, and a rebuild procedure organised around preserving state across the deletion of the bucket that holds it. Begoug et al. (2023) found deployment pipelines among the hardest IaC topics on Stack Overflow; this study suggests that much of that difficulty may be state management rather than pipeline syntax."),
p("The third barrier is time spent destroying, discussed in Section 5.9. The fourth is the absence of review, discussed above. Taken together, the themes suggest that the technical barriers to replacing manual console work with Terraform in a pipeline were solvable by one engineer in a short time, while the governance barrier, getting a second person to approve changes, was not addressed at all. That is consistent with the facilitating-conditions argument in Section 2.3.4: review needs a second person and a rule that enforces it, and neither is supplied by the tooling."),
h2("5.6 Integration and Meta-Inferences"),
p("Three meta-inferences follow from the joint display (Table 4.12). First, where the strands converge (H1, H3 and destroy time), the quantitative result is explained by a specific mechanism in the record: fast, consistent builds because the code could be re-applied unchanged; reliable rebuilds because state was handled deliberately; slow destroys because service stacks are removed one after another. Second, where the strands complement each other (RQ1 and H4), the qualitative strand shows what the number hides: 79% coverage coexists with 0% review because the workflow was built for one person. Third, where they diverge from the proposal (H2 and H5), the record explains why the planned evidence does not exist. All inferences about the manual condition remain conditional on the simulation."),
h2("5.7 Revisiting the Conceptual Framework"),
p("Figure 5.1 shows the framework revised in the light of the findings. Two paths are supported conditional on the simulated manual condition (method to duration and method to reproducibility); one is supported in part (method to coverage, without review); and two remain untested (method to drift, and the moderating effect of depth). The evidence also suggests two refinements. First, review should be separated from coverage as an outcome in its own right, since the study shows they can diverge completely. Second, the framework should include destruction duration alongside provisioning duration, because destruction dominated the cycle and limits how often environments can be rebuilt."),
("fig", "fig5_1_revised_framework.png", 6.0),
figcap("Figure 5.1. Revised conceptual framework based on the integrated findings. Dashed grey elements were not tested; annotations state the evidence for each path."),
h2("5.8 Comparison with Prior Studies"),
caption("Table 5.1", "Comparison of Present Findings with Prior Studies"),
table(["Finding in this study", "Prior study", "Relationship"], [
    [f"Terraform build CV {CV:.1f}%; no failed rebuild in {N}", "Regvart et al. (2026): Terraform SD 8.42 s on 253 s; no failure in 30", "Consistent, on the same number of cycles"],
    [f"Back-to-back runs built {(TRG['test']['ratio'] - 1) * 100:.1f}% slower; no trend over 30 runs", "Regvart et al. (2026): per-tool timings with low dispersion", "Extends: conditions outside the code add a few per cent of variation"],
    [f"Destroy took {DES / TF:.1f} times as long as create", "Regvart et al. (2026): destroy slower than create for every tool", "Consistent; larger gap here, attributed to sequential service-stack deletion"],
    [f"Terraform {RATIO:.1f}× faster than simulated manual build", "No prior study compares Terraform with manual provisioning", "New, conditional on the simulation"],
    ["Encryption at rest applied; TLS and WAF absent", "Verdet et al. (2025): access control applied, encryption at rest neglected", "Partly consistent: security that needs external prerequisites is what gets left out"],
    ["Provider constrained to a major version; lock file not committed", "Begoug, Ouni, and Chouchen (2025); Begoug, Ouni, and Khelifi (2025): lag of 9–10 months", "Consistent risk: versions can drift between cycles"],
    ["Account identifiers hard-coded in image and bucket names", "Rahman & Williams (2019): hard-coded strings associated with defects", "Consistent"],
    ["Commit-push-wait loop; failed report commit found only by running", "Rostami Mazrae et al. (2023): workflows cannot be tested locally", "Consistent"],
    ["Pipeline triggered by push; no pull requests", "Decan et al. (2022): push the most common trigger", "Consistent; extends the finding to infrastructure"],
    ["Coverage rose without review", "Hilton et al. (2016); Vasilescu et al. (2015): CI benefits via pull requests", "Divergent context: single engineer, no review route"],
    ["State management the hardest engineering problem", "Begoug et al. (2023): deployment pipelines among the hardest topics", "Extends: identifies state as a source of that difficulty"],
], [3000, 3240, 2400], sz=18),
h2("5.9 Unexpected Findings"),
p(f"Two findings were not anticipated. The first is that destruction, not creation, dominated the cycle: {DES:.1f} of {CYC:.1f} minutes, with {R['destroy_deploy_share_pct']:.1f}% of the cycle spent destroying the five service stacks. Deleting an ECS service waits for its tasks to drain and stop, and the rebuild script destroys the stacks sequentially, so the waits add up. The literature measures provisioning far more often than removal, yet for an organisation that wants disposable test environments or fast disaster-recovery rehearsal, removal time sets the pace. It also matters for the comparison with manual work: a manual teardown of 140 resources would face the same AWS waits plus the hands-on time, so the advantage of automation is likely to be at least as large on the destroy side."),
p("The second is that the hardest moment of the pipeline campaign had nothing to do with provisioning. Run 3's infrastructure work was not the problem; the step that committed the report and the remote-state file back to the repository failed because of a race with the previous run's commit. The pipeline that replaced manual console work had itself become software that needed debugging, which is the commit-push-wait cost Rostami Mazrae et al. (2023) describe, transferred from application CI to infrastructure."),
h2("5.10 Chapter Summary"),
p(f"The discussion has shown that Terraform executed by GitHub Actions made the architecture fast ({TF:.1f} minutes) and reliable to rebuild, consistent with the closest prior study, while leaving review entirely to human practice. Against a simulated manual baseline, H1 and H3 are supported, conditional on the model; H4 is supported for coverage but not review; H2 and H5 remain untested, for reasons the implementation record explains. The barriers lay less in Terraform syntax than in state management, destruction time and governance. The revised framework separates review from coverage and adds destruction time. Chapter 6 draws the conclusions and recommendations."),
]
